    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To find \\(\\sin F\\) in the right triangle \\(DEF\\) where \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\) and \\(\\sin E = 1\\), we start by noting that \\(\\sin E = 1\\) implies that angle \\(E\\) is a right angle (since the sine of a right angle is always 1). Therefore, the triangle \\(DEF\\) is a right triangle with \\(E = 90^\\circ\\).\n\nIn a right triangle, the sum of the non-right angles is \\(90^\\circ\\), so \\(F = 90^\\circ - D\\). Using the fact that \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we know from the Pythagorean identity that:\n\n\\[\n\\sin^2 D + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\sin D = \\frac{5}{13}\\), we get:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{5}{13}\\right)^2 + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\nThis simplifies to:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{25}{169} + \\cos^2 D = 1\n\\]\n\nSubtracting \\(\\frac{25}{169}\\) from both sides, we find:\n\n\\[\n\\cos^2 D = 1 - \\frac{25}{169} = \\frac{169}{169} - \\frac{25}{169} = \\frac{144}{169}\n\\]\n\nTaking the square root of both sides, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\cos D = \\pm \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(D\\) is an angle in a right triangle, it must be between \\(0^\\circ\\) and \\(90^\\circ\\), so \\(\\cos D\\) must be positive. Therefore, \\(\\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\\).\n\nSince \\(F = 90^\\circ - D\\), we have:\n\n\\[\n\\sin F = \\sin (90^\\circ - D) = \\cos D = \\frac{12}{13}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(\\sin F\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{12}{13}}\n\\]",
